{
  "mechanism": {
    "summary": "Each request maps to a key (API key plus route group) and is checked against a token bucket in a shared store: token count, last-refill timestamp, capacity and refill rate. An atomic script reads the count and timestamp, adds the tokens earned since then up to the capacity, spends a token if any remain and writes the state back, so all gateway nodes share one limit. Refusals get HTTP 429 with Retry-After. Because the check is a network round trip, a slow store forces a choice: fail open, fail closed, or per-instance fallback.",
    "sourceChunkIds": [
      "source-1#chunk-1",
      "source-1#chunk-2",
      "source-2#chunk-2"
    ],
    "steps": [
      {
        "text": "Build the limiter key from the API key and the route group, so each caller gets a limit per route.",
        "sourceChunkIds": [
          "source-1#chunk-1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Read the token count and the last-refill timestamp for that key from the shared store.",
        "sourceChunkIds": [
          "source-1#chunk-1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Add the tokens earned since that timestamp (elapsed seconds multiplied by the refill rate), capped at the bucket's capacity.",
        "sourceChunkIds": [
          "source-1#chunk-1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "If a token is left, spend it, write the new count and timestamp back, and let the request through.",
        "sourceChunkIds": [
          "source-1#chunk-1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Otherwise refuse with HTTP 429 and compute Retry-After from the refill rate.",
        "sourceChunkIds": [
          "source-1#chunk-1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "Run read, compute and write as a single atomic script inside the store, so two nodes cannot both spend the last token.",
        "sourceChunkIds": [
          "source-1#chunk-1",
          "source-1#chunk-2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "If the store call exceeds the configured timeout, apply the failure policy: fail closed with 503, fail open, or fall back to per-instance limits divided across instances.",
        "sourceChunkIds": [
          "source-2#chunk-2",
          "source-3#chunk-2"
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "plainSummary": "Each incoming request asks the rate limiter one question: may this caller go now? A token bucket is a small pile of passes kept for each caller. Each request spends one pass, and passes trickle back in at a steady rate. All gateway machines check the same pile, kept in one shared store. When the store gets slow, the limiter must refuse requests or let too many through.",
  "analogies": [
    {
      "analogy": "A club keeps a bowl of guest passes at the door: each visitor takes one, and a helper quietly drops more into the bowl at a steady trickle.",
      "matches": "The bowl is the bucket, its maximum size is the capacity, the helper's trickle is the refill rate, and a visitor who finds the bowl empty is turned away — exactly the token bucket decision.",
      "breaksDown": "The bowl sits at the door where a bouncer can see it; the real bucket lives in a shared store every gateway reaches over the network, and that trip is where things go wrong."
    },
    {
      "analogy": "A chain of shops honours a head-office coupon; when head office's line goes down, each shop decides for itself and the same coupon gets used at every branch.",
      "matches": "That is the local fallback: with the shared store unreachable, each gateway instance enforces the limit on its own, so the effective limit becomes the limit times the number of instances.",
      "breaksDown": "The shops are gateway instances whose limits are meant to add up to one contract, and the store is usually slow rather than gone, so the split-brain lasts only as long as the latency."
    }
  ],
  "glossary": [
    {
      "term": "Rate limiter",
      "plainMeaning": "A component that decides, per request, whether this caller may do this now.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "Token bucket",
      "plainMeaning": "A per-key allowance with a maximum size (capacity) and a steady refill rate; each request spends a token.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1",
        "source-2#chunk-1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "Shared store",
      "plainMeaning": "The one in-memory store that every gateway node reads and writes the bucket state in, so all nodes enforce the same limit.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "Atomic script",
      "plainMeaning": "A read-compute-write sequence the store runs as one indivisible unit, so no other node can interleave with it.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "Retry-After",
      "plainMeaning": "Response header on a refusal that says how many seconds until the next token arrives.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "Fixed window",
      "plainMeaning": "Counting requests per calendar minute and resetting the counter at the top of each minute.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "Sliding window log",
      "plainMeaning": "Storing the timestamp of every request in the window and counting them; exact, but memory grows with the limit.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "Sliding window counter",
      "plainMeaning": "Keeping only the current and previous window counts and weighting the previous by how much of it still overlaps; cheap but approximate.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "Fail closed",
      "plainMeaning": "On a store failure, refuse the request (503) rather than serve traffic the limiter cannot count.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "Fail open",
      "plainMeaning": "On a store failure, let the request through uncounted, so the limit stops being enforced.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-2",
        "source-3#chunk-2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "Local fallback",
      "plainMeaning": "Each instance enforces the limit on its own while the store is down, multiplying the effective limit by the instance count.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "term": "Store timeout",
      "plainMeaning": "How long a store call may take before it counts as a store failure and the failure policy kicks in.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-2"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    {
      "id": "fact-1",
      "statement": "A rate limiter decides, per request, whether a caller may do this now; a token bucket decides from state kept per key: token count, last-refill timestamp, capacity and refill rate.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1",
        "source-2#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-2",
      "statement": "Each request takes a token; when the bucket is empty the request is refused with HTTP 429 Too Many Requests and a Retry-After header giving the seconds until the next token arrives.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-3",
      "statement": "At Hollowmere, the invented gateway in the source, a free-tier key gets a capacity of 100 tokens and a refill rate of 10 tokens per second: a client that has been quiet can burst 100 requests at once, then settles to 10 requests per second.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-4",
      "statement": "The limiter key is built from the API key plus the route group, so a caller is limited per route rather than globally.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-5",
      "statement": "The check is a read of token count and last-refill timestamp, an add of elapsed seconds times the refill rate capped at capacity, a spend of a token and a write of the new count and timestamp back to the store.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-6",
      "statement": "Read, compute and write run as one atomic script inside the store; without that, two gateway nodes can read the same count, both see a token left, and both let a request through.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-1",
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-7",
      "statement": "Hollowmere runs 24 gateway nodes behind a load balancer; with a bucket per node, a client spread across all of them would get 24 times its limit.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-8",
      "statement": "Keeping the bucket in one shared in-memory store costs a network round trip on every request: 0.4 ms at the median and 2.1 ms at p99 in the source's measurements from June 2024.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-9",
      "statement": "The first version used a fixed window: counting requests per calendar minute and resetting at the top of each minute, cheap because it needed a single counter per key.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-10",
      "statement": "A fixed window lets a client send its whole quota in the last second of a minute and again in the first second of the next, up to 2 times the limit across the boundary; the token bucket was chosen because it bounds bursts by the capacity no matter where the clock is.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-11",
      "statement": "A rate limiter protects fairness between well-behaved clients, not the service from a flood: a flood of 2 million requests per second still reaches the gateways, which must parse every request and look up its key before they can refuse it; dropping that traffic is the network edge's job.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-1#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-12",
      "statement": "quotaflow ships token bucket, sliding window log and sliding window counter behind a single interface and keeps state in a shared store, so every instance of a service enforces the same limit.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-13",
      "statement": "The sliding window log stores a timestamp per request: exact, but memory grows with the limit — 8 bytes per timestamp, about 8 KB per active key, around 4 GB across 500,000 active keys.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-14",
      "statement": "The sliding window counter keeps only the current and previous window counts and weights the previous one by how much of it still overlaps; it needs a couple of counters per key and is approximate because it assumes requests in the previous window were evenly spread.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-15",
      "statement": "quotaflow's default store timeout is 50 ms — a slower call counts as a store failure — and its default on failure is fail closed: refuse with 503 rather than serve traffic it cannot count, a default the README recommends keeping for every route.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-16",
      "statement": "quotaflow's local fallback is off by default; when enabled, each instance enforces the limit on its own while the store is down, which multiplies the effective limit by the number of instances.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-17",
      "statement": "Clock skew between instances does not affect quotaflow, because timestamps are taken inside the store; idle keys are not expired until some windows after their last request, so their memory lingers briefly.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-18",
      "statement": "In the Larkspur Pay incident, the limiter's shared store became slow, the limiter fell back to local limits on each of the 40 checkout instances, the payment provider received far more than its contract allowed and throttled all traffic; 31% of checkout requests failed for 47 minutes.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-3#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-19",
      "statement": "A routine failover moved the limiter's store to a replica in another zone and store calls went from about 1 ms to 80 ms.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-3#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-20",
      "statement": "Calls over the store timeout then counted as failures; the limiter was configured to fail open, so each instance enforced the limit locally and traffic to the payment provider reached roughly 40 times its contracted rate of 300 requests per second.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-3#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-21",
      "statement": "The provider began answering 429 to most calls and checkout errors rose to 31%; when the store moved back to its home zone the limits were shared again and the errors stopped.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-3#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-22",
      "statement": "Fail open was chosen for checkout deliberately — refusing payments because the limiter's store is slow loses sales on every request — which held for their own servers with headroom but not for the downstream provider, whose limit is fixed by contract.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-3#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-23",
      "statement": "After the incident the payment-provider limiter fails closed, local fallback limits are divided by the number of instances so the instances together stay within the contracted rate, and the store timeout rose to 150 ms, above a cross-zone round trip.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-3#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-24",
      "statement": "The report leaves one item open: whether limits for their own APIs should fail open or closed is undecided, teams disagree, and the trade-off depends on what sits behind each route.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-3#chunk-2"
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
    {
      "id": "fact-25",
      "statement": "The quotaflow README claims it is the fastest and most accurate rate limiter available, with accuracy within a stated margin of an ideal limiter under any load; no benchmark or test conditions are given, so it is a marketing claim, not a measurement.",
      "sourceChunkIds": [
        "source-2#chunk-1"
      ],
      "confidence": "low"
    }
  ],
